1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,567
JAMIE: Meta's AI chatbot just got twenty thousand Instagram accounts stolen, and the security feature designed to stop exactly this was the hole the hackers climbed through.

2
00:00:08,567 --> 00:00:24,115
ALEX: So the market's supposed to believe AI is infrastructure now—except Meta's chatbot got turned into a credential harvester by its own security filter, which means the infrastructure is actively stealing from its users. That's like selling someone a home security system that doubles as a door for burglars.

3
00:00:24,115 --> 00:00:57,433
ALEX: Markets are up across the board—S&P at seven thousand four hundred forty-eight, up zero point eighty-seven percent. Nasdaq hit twenty-six thousand one hundred eight, up one point five five percent. But here's where it gets interesting: the strength is almost entirely in semiconductors. A M D jumped five point zero four percent, Broadcom up three point two five percent. Meanwhile, the big tech names are bleeding. Microsoft down one point three three percent, Google down one point seven five percent. The V I X cratered fourteen and a half points to eighteen point three eight, which usually means investors think the coast is clear.

4
00:00:57,433 --> 00:01:03,145
JAMIE: So chips are flying and the mega-cap tech is tanking. That's not a rally, that's a reshuffling.

5
00:01:03,145 --> 00:01:24,722
MAYA: Right, and the V I X floor-dive feels premature given we just spent the first segment talking about Meta's own AI security system turning into a credential-stealing machine. Like, the market's convinced everything's fine while the actual infrastructure companies are actively compromised. Tesla's up two point eighty-five percent, Eli Lilly up two point five four—those are the safe-haven trades. Nobody's panicking, but nobody's actually fixing anything either.

6
00:01:24,722 --> 00:01:48,521
MAYA: So the actual mechanism here—Meta's chatbot asks you security questions to verify your identity before it resets your password, right? Except the questions are laughably easy. 'What's your favorite food?' 'What city were you born in?' The kind of stuff you've already posted publicly or that's sitting in a breached database somewhere. Hackers just scraped that data, fed it to the chatbot, and it handed over the account recovery link like a vending machine.

7
00:01:48,521 --> 00:02:15,492
ALEX: The thing that kills me is Meta's pitch for this feature was fraud prevention. They told users the chatbot would catch phishing attempts. What they built instead was a phishing machine with a Meta logo on it. The chatbot didn't just fail at security—it became the attack vector. It's the only way to describe it: a company shipped a product that does the exact opposite of what it claims to do, at scale, and nobody caught it before twenty thousand accounts were already gone.

8
00:02:15,492 --> 00:02:19,300
JAMIE: How long was this live before Meta even knew about it?

9
00:02:19,300 --> 00:02:41,512
MAYA: They didn't know. A researcher had to report it. Meta only confirmed it when they filed the Maine breach notice—which is a regulatory filing, not a press release. They weren't going to volunteer this information. So this feature was sitting there, actively compromising accounts, and Meta's security team was either not monitoring their own infrastructure or didn't understand what their own AI was doing. Pick whichever scares you more.

10
00:02:41,512 --> 00:02:58,012
ALEX: Household financial anxiety just hit its highest level since July twenty twenty-two. New York Fed survey came out this morning—inflation expectations are basically flat, but the general perception of conditions got worse. So rate cuts are priced in, the Fed's supposed to start cutting soon, and people are somehow more worried.

11
00:02:58,012 --> 00:03:16,099
MAYA: That's because rate cuts don't fix the fact that rent is already five hundred dollars higher than it was two years ago. The Fed can drop rates to zero and your landlord still isn't retroactively charging you less. People aren't anxious about the future direction of policy—they're anxious about the present direction of their checking account.

12
00:03:16,099 --> 00:03:25,301
JAMIE: United Airlines CEO just said there's nothing to airline mergers after American rejected a deal. Which is exactly what he said before the last three consolidation waves happened.

13
00:03:25,301 --> 00:03:37,994
ALEX: The quote was literally 'There's nothing.' Not 'We're not interested.' Not 'The timing isn't right.' He said there is nothing. Which in CEO language means 'The lawyers told me not to confirm anything until the paperwork is already signed.'

14
00:03:37,994 --> 00:03:53,542
MAYA: Uber, Wayve, and Waymo are all racing robotaxis in London right now. The same city that banned Uber five years ago is now hosting three different self-driving futures simultaneously. It's like London said, 'We rejected your company, but we'll absolutely let you test your autonomous vehicle experiment here.'
  ↳ source: https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/08/uber-wayve-and-waymo-are-headed-towards-a-robotaxi-showdown-in-london/

15
00:03:53,542 --> 00:04:04,965
JAMIE: So Uber customers can sign up for an interest list to get matched with a Wayve robotaxi. That's not a product launch—that's a lottery ticket for people who want to find out if they're brave enough.
  ↳ source: https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/08/uber-wayve-and-waymo-are-headed-towards-a-robotaxi-showdown-in-london/

16
00:04:04,965 --> 00:04:17,340
MAYA: WhatsApp caught N S O spyware again. Same group. After a court order explicitly told them to stop. At some point 'caught' is just code for 'announced we found it again while we were looking for something else.'

17
00:04:17,340 --> 00:04:34,793
ALEX: N S O Group violated a court order to conduct a phishing campaign targeting WhatsApp users with their spyware. That's not a security vulnerability—that's a court order with no teeth. WhatsApp disrupted the attack, but the fact that N S O is openly defying judicial orders and still operating tells you everything about enforcement.

18
00:04:34,793 --> 00:04:49,072
JAMIE: On the tape tomorrow: Campbell Soup and Motorcar Parts both reporting on June eighth. Neither one's a household name anymore, but that's exactly why earnings matter—these are the companies the market stopped paying attention to, which means when they miss, nobody sees it coming.

19
00:04:49,072 --> 00:05:10,014
ALEX: Campbell's has been trying to rebrand away from condensed soup for a decade. Motorcar Parts supplies O E M components to the big automakers. Both of them live in a world where input costs are sticky and pricing power is nonexistent. If either one guides down, you're watching a company admit that the cost of doing business just outran their ability to pass it along.

20
00:05:10,014 --> 00:05:17,947
ALEX: Meta built a bouncer that checks IDs by reading your diary out loud, then hands the keys to anyone who guesses your favorite food.

21
00:05:17,947 --> 00:05:23,342
MAYA: And they're still wondering why people are filling out breach notices instead of downloading the app.

22
00:05:23,342 --> 00:05:27,784
JAMIE: This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
